// lifetime: the heap probe that the driver lifetime tests share.
//
// A run of the driver owns its session and its analysis. It releases both on
// every path out. No other test sees a release that is missing: the
// gate sanitizers instrument the C compiler and not the x86-64 program that
// the harness runs.
//
// The heap sees one. Many runs of one command line ask for the same blocks
// in the same order. A run that gives the blocks back leaves the program
// break where it was. A run that keeps one block pushes the break up by the
// leaked bytes of each round.
//
// The probe watches two views, because each view is blind to what the other
// view sees. The program break does not move for a leak that the allocator
// serves out of a free chunk it already holds. The address of a fresh block
// does not move for a large leak: an address probe read 0 with
// `analysis_free` deleted, while the break had climbed by megabytes. The
// answer is the larger of the two distances.
//
// Two views are necessary and not sufficient. A round must also be large
// enough for the release that it claims to witness. A deleted `del(ck->syms)`
// leaks about 144 bytes for each analysis. Two hundred rounds of that stay
// inside the 132 KB that glibc gives a process at startup, so neither view
// moves and the probe reads 0. ROUNDS is therefore above the point where the
// smallest release becomes visible. A release added later gets the same
// standard: delete it, watch the test go red, and increase ROUNDS until the
// test goes red.
//
// Neither view sees a leak of a block above 128 KB. glibc serves such an
// allocation by mmap, so the program break does not move, and a round that is
// large enough to need one is also a round that makes the block view useless.
// The emitter mode measured that: its module is 226998 bytes, its buffer for
// the module is an mmap block, and a deletion of the release of that buffer
// leaves the emitter test green. The closure mode emits about two kilobytes
// and is the witness of that one release. So a release is held by the mode
// whose round keeps it inside the heap.
//
// Each mode of the driver is a test file of its own, and this module is what
// those files share. One mode in each program keeps every program below the
// 60 s that the harness gives a program (toolchain.md 7.3). One mode in each
// program also gives each mode a heap that no earlier mode fragmented: the
// measurement is a measurement of the allocator, so the first act of each
// program is its own loop.
//
// D3.10: this module uses no function-pointer dispatch table
// D20.5: an analysis repeated must not grow the heap

import capture;
import driver;
import std.io;
import std.sys;

/// The rounds of a front-end mode, and the distance in bytes that fails it.
///
/// A leak of about 144 bytes for each round needs about nine hundred rounds to
/// push the break out of the heap that the process starts with. The early
/// sample comes at half the rounds. The count is therefore well above twice
/// nine hundred.
u64 ROUNDS = 4000;
u64 BOUND = 4096;

/// How many of the last rounds the probe holds against the early round. The
/// allocator does not hand a round the same small block every time: over the
/// emitter program the address runs through a cycle of eight, so two late
/// samples did not find one round in step with the early round and the probe
/// read 26512 with nothing leaked. Sixteen covers that cycle two times. A leak
/// moves every sample, so the smallest distance costs nothing.
u64 LATE_SAMPLES = 16;

/// The exit status that a refused program leaves. The mode that compiles a
/// refused program asserts this status of its first round.
/// D14.1
i32 EXIT_COMPILE_ERROR = 1;

/// The current program break, which is the end of the heap. sbrk(0) moves
/// nothing and reports the break. std has no binding for it and these tests are
/// its only callers.
/// D9.8
extern fn void* sbrk(i64 increment);

fn u64 probe_address() {
    return cast(sbrk(0), u64);
}

/// The other view: the address that a small fresh block gets. It moves when a
/// round kept a block that the next round would have got. The probe releases
/// the block at once, so the probe itself leaks nothing.
u64 PROBE_BLOCK = 32;

fn u64 probe_block() {
    u8 mut@ own block = new(u8, PROBE_BLOCK);
    u64 at = cast(block.ptr, u64);
    del(block);
    return at;
}

/// Measures a front-end mode over ROUNDS rounds, with both views on.
fn u64 measure(string@ args) {
    return measure_rounds(args, ROUNDS, true);
}

/// The same over a round count of its own, and with the block view under a
/// switch. The emitter mode compiles a much larger program, so it runs fewer
/// rounds and its program is rich enough that every release it witnesses is
/// the size of a round and not the size of a block. notes/testing.md 4 states
/// that rule.
///
/// The block view is off for the emitter mode, and the reason is a
/// measurement. A round that allocates a hundred kilobytes and gives it back
/// leaves the heap with free chunks of every size, so the address that a
/// 32-byte probe gets lands where one of those chunks starts: it moved through
/// a range of 450 KB over rounds that leaked nothing, and no window or cycle
/// makes it repeat. The break is the view that answers for that mode, and it
/// answers for all of it: each of the sixteen releases there is at least a
/// kilobyte for each round, so a missing release pushes the break out by
/// hundreds of kilobytes. The block view stays on for the four front-end modes
/// and for the index mode, where it is steady and where it is the only view
/// that sees a small release.
fn u64 measure_rounds(string@ args, u64 rounds, bool watch_block) {
    return measure_status(args, rounds, watch_block, 0);
}

/// The same, for a mode whose command line ends in a diagnostic: the program
/// of the checked mode is refused by the checker, so its rounds exit 1 and a
/// round that exits 0 is the broken one.
fn u64 measure_status(string@ args, u64 rounds, bool watch_block, i32 want_status) {
    u64 mut early = 0;
    u64 mut early_block = 0;
    u64 mut broke = 0;
    u64 mut block = 0;
    bool mut sampled = false;
    u64 mut i = 0;
    while (i < rounds) {
        // The driver writes to both descriptors. The test captures them, so
        // that the stdout of the test is its one line.
        i32 saved_out = capture.begin(io.STDOUT, "out.txt");
        i32 saved_err = capture.begin(io.STDERR, "err.txt");
        i32 status = driver.run(args);
        capture.end(io.STDERR, saved_err);
        capture.end(io.STDOUT, saved_out);
        if (i == 0) {
            // The first round answers for the rest. A round that stops early
            // does not make the allocations of a pass that the probe claims
            // to watch, and a probe that watches nothing is green. The
            // emitter mode ran for a whole afternoon over a program with a
            // type error: `-S` stopped at the front end, and every deletion
            // of a release in gen.gen_free was unwitnessed.
            assert(status == want_status);
        }
        if (i == rounds / 2) {
            early = probe_address();
            early_block = probe_block();
        }
        if (i + LATE_SAMPLES >= rounds) {
            u64 broke_now = distance(probe_address(), early);
            u64 mut block_now = 0;
            if (watch_block) {
                block_now = distance(probe_block(), early_block);
            }
            if (!sampled || broke_now < broke) {
                broke = broke_now;
            }
            if (!sampled || block_now < block) {
                block = block_now;
            }
            sampled = true;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    assert(early != 0 && early_block != 0 && sampled);
    // A move of either view is a leak, so the answer is the larger distance.
    if (broke > block) {
        return broke;
    }
    return block;
}

/// How far apart two addresses are, whichever way the allocator moved. A leak
/// that hands a later round a lower address is a leak too, and a subtraction
/// the wrong way round traps before the assertion.
/// D11.1
fn u64 distance(u64 a, u64 b) {
    if (a > b) {
        return a - b;
    }
    return b - a;
}

fn void write_file(string path, string text) {
    assert(io.write_file(path, cast(text, u8@)));
}

/// Creates one directory, which may already exist.
extern fn i32 mkdir(char* path, u32 mode);

/// The errno that a directory which already exists reports (stdlib.md 2.2).
i32 EEXIST = 17;

/// Every closure holds `std.rt`. The driver reads it from `<std-dir>/rt.ft` and
/// looks for that directory in `std` beside the program when nothing names one.
/// toolchain.md 1 states where it looks. The runs give `fort` as argv[0], so
/// the directory is `std` of the working directory of the test. The driver
/// needs a file there that parses, and not a runtime: these tests run nothing
/// that they build.
fn void write_runtime() {
    assert(mkdir("std".ptr, 0o700) == 0 || sys.errno() == EEXIST);
    write_file("std/rt.ft", "// The empty runtime of a driver test.\n");
}
